---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import SchemaOnReadFlow from "../components/SchemaOnReadFlow.astro";

const outputs = [
  ["Dashboard", "Materialize or query a stable order table with ordinary dimensions and measures."],
  ["Investigation", "Change the temporal relationship or projected features for a one-off question."],
  ["Workflow", "Return the exact population and columns needed for an operational action."],
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Schema on Read — Activity Schema"
  description="How Activity Schema pivots a narrow event stream into conventional BI tables with a stable grain, projected features, and temporal context."
  path="/schema-on-read/"
>
  <header class="page-hero shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">Schema on read</p>
    <h1>Model the events once. Shape the table when you need it.</h1>
    <p class="lede">The activity stream is a staging and semantic layer. It is excellent for a chronological customer journey. For BI, reporting, and workflows, <strong>choose a grain, pivot the relevant features, and return an ordinary table.</strong></p>
  </header>

  <section class="section-tight"><div class="shell"><SchemaOnReadFlow /></div></section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">A stable contract underneath</p><h2>Schema on read does not mean meaning on read.</h2></div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Every activity is modeled in advance with a stable name, entity, timestamp, identifier, and activity-specific feature structure. Rows for the same activity should expose a consistent set of top-level <code>feature_json</code> keys.</p>
        <p>What remains flexible is the analytical shape. A completed-order table can project order total, coupon, and product; append the last marketing session before the order; and join reusable product reference data without changing the source activities.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section bi-section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">For BI tools</p><h2>Give the tool the wide result—not the modeling puzzle.</h2></div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Looker, Tableau, Power BI, spreadsheets, and reporting applications generally work best when the result already has a declared row grain and named columns. The query layer can expose that result as a logical view, a cached query, or a materialized table depending on freshness, reuse, cost, and warehouse behavior.</p>
        <p>The BI tool does not need to understand every JSON structure or temporal self-join. It consumes the compiled result just as it would any other warehouse table.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="shell output-grid">
      {outputs.map(([title, description]) => <article><h3>{title}</h3><p>{description}</p></article>)}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section choice-section">
    <div class="shell choice-grid">
      <div><span class="mono">01</span><h3>Logical view</h3><p>Compile when consumed. Useful for changing questions and modest reuse.</p></div>
      <div><span class="mono">02</span><h3>Cached result</h3><p>Reuse an unchanged answer when the warehouse or BI layer can safely serve it.</p></div>
      <div><span class="mono">03</span><h3>Incremental table</h3><p>Persist a high-use result and rebuild only the affected time window when the assumptions permit it.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section next-section">
    <div class="shell split"><div><p class="eyebrow">Next</p><h2>See why this shape can be maintained efficiently.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>Efficiency depends on bytes scanned, partitioning, update behavior, reuse, and how often a result is actually consumed.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button button-primary" href="efficiency/">Read the Efficiency guide <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a class="button button-secondary" href="querying/">Review the query grammar</a></div></div></div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .split { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: clamp(48px, 8vw, 100px); }
  .bi-section { background: var(--white); }
  .output-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1.1fr 1fr 0.9fr; gap: 20px; margin-top: 72px; }
  .output-grid article { min-height: 220px; padding: 24px; border: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .output-grid p, .choice-grid p { margin: 0; color: var(--steel); }
  .choice-section { background: var(--midnight); }
  .choice-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); }
  .choice-grid > div { min-height: 280px; padding: 28px; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.16); border-right: 0; }
  .choice-grid > div:last-child { border-right: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.16); }
  .choice-grid span { display: block; margin-bottom: 92px; color: var(--seafoam); font-size: .68rem; }
  .choice-grid h3 { color: var(--white); }
  .choice-grid p { color: rgba(255,255,255,.65); }
  @media (max-width: 760px) { .split, .output-grid, .choice-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } .choice-grid > div { border-right: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.16); border-bottom: 0; } .choice-grid > div:last-child { border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.16); } }
</style>
